<pre><h1>    Exercise Name: Triceps Cable Pushdown</h1></pre>
    <u><b><h4>Exercise Overview</h4></b></u>
    Target Muscle Activation


 Primary: Triceps Brachii (Lateral Head, Medial Head, and Long Head)
 Secondary: Core / Abs (for body stabilization) & Forearms (grip)

</div></div>


<h1>Step-by-Step Execution Guide</h1>

<u><b><h4>1. Setup:</h4></b></u>
 Attach a straight bar or V-bar to a high pulley on a cable machine.
 Stand facing the machine with feet hip-width apart or in a slightly staggered stance for balance.
 Grab the bar with an overhand grip (palms facing down) roughly shoulder-width apart.
 Pull the bar down so your upper arms are tucked close to the sides of your torso, keeping your elbows locked in place at roughly a 90-degree angle.


<u><b><h4>2. The Pushdown (Concentric Phase):</h4></b></u>
 Exhale and push the bar straight down toward your thighs by extending your elbows.
 Keep your upper arms completely fixed against your body—do not let your elbows drift forward or flare outward.
 Squeeze your triceps tightly at the bottom when your arms are fully extended.


<u><b><h4>3. The Return (Eccentric Phase):</h4></b></u>
 Inhale and slowly raise the bar back up to about chest height, bending at the elbows in a controlled manner.
 Stop at roughly a 90-degree bend at the elbows to maintain continuous tension on the triceps without swinging.





<h1>Key Benefits</h1>

 <b>◉Constant Cable Tension:</b> Unlike dumbbells, cables provide continuous tension throughout the entire range of motion, maximizing muscle activation.<br>

<b>◉Isolated Arm Growth:</b> Isolates the triceps without relying on shoulder or chest momentum, helping build size and definition on the back of the arms.<br>

 <b>◉Joint-Friendly:</b> Easy to adjust the weight and safer on the elbows compared to heavy free-weight extension exercises like skull crushers.<br>

 <b>◉Core & Grip Strength:</b> Requires static core support and grip strength to maintain good form against the pull of the cable stack.


<h1>Recommended Sets & Repetitions</h1>

 Hypertrophy (Muscle Building): 3–4 sets of 10–15 reps with controlled resistance.
 Endurance & Toning: 3 sets of 15–20 reps with lighter weight.
 Heavy Strength: 3–4 sets of 8–10 reps.
 Rest: 60 seconds between sets.

